      "id": 1,
      "text": "Saint Joseph was named the seat of Berrien County in 1894 after Berrien Springs relinquished that status.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Joseph%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city lies on Lake Michigan at the mouth of the Saint Joseph River, a key water route between the Great Lakes and Mississippi.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Joseph%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1870 the Chicago and Michigan Lake Shore Railroad extended a line to Saint Joseph, connecting it to Grand Rapids, Muskegon, Detroit and Chicago.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Joseph%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The United States Lifesaving Service built a Lifesaving Station in 1876 at Saint Joseph, appointing Joseph Napier as its first stationkeeper.",
